1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,657
Before you type, our triage page shows
a serif greeting asking where it hurts,

2
00:00:04,657 --> 00:00:09,195
an input box in the middle of the screen,
and a row of suggestions under it.

3
00:00:09,515 --> 00:00:11,947
If that reminds you of Claude's app,
it should.

4
00:00:12,267 --> 00:00:15,531
You asked for a one-to-one copy,
and the agent built it.

5
00:00:15,767 --> 00:00:18,711
Isn't copying exactly
what a product should avoid?

6
00:00:19,031 --> 00:00:20,204
Here it was the plan.

7
00:00:20,524 --> 00:00:23,484
What was copied,
what was refused, what we added,

8
00:00:23,484 --> 00:00:26,625
and what nobody has built yet:
that is this episode.

9
00:00:27,967 --> 00:00:29,140
First, the parts.

10
00:00:29,460 --> 00:00:33,748
Almost every chat app has the same ones,
and designers have names for them.

11
00:00:34,068 --> 00:00:37,140
The empty state is the page
before the first message.

12
00:00:37,460 --> 00:00:41,898
Suggestion chips are ready-made questions;
ours start demo replays.

13
00:00:42,218 --> 00:00:44,180
The composer is the input box.

14
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:49,812
Inside it sit a model menu and the send key,
which turns into a stop key while you wait.

15
00:00:50,050 --> 00:00:53,277
Once you send,
your words sit in a bubble on the right,

16
00:00:53,277 --> 00:00:55,917
and the answer runs
full width without a box.

17
00:00:56,237 --> 00:01:00,994
Under each answer is an action row:
copy, and under the latest one, retry.

18
00:01:01,314 --> 00:01:04,962
Scroll up, and a round button appears
to take you back to the bottom.

19
00:01:05,282 --> 00:01:08,824
And under the input, one line of fine print:
the disclaimer.

20
00:01:11,367 --> 00:01:13,074
The team has no designer.

21
00:01:13,394 --> 00:01:16,381
You asked for the page
to follow Claude's chat,

22
00:01:16,381 --> 00:01:21,213
one to one, and that night the agent
wrote the reasons down as decision six,

23
00:01:21,213 --> 00:01:22,866
against two other options.

24
00:01:23,186 --> 00:01:24,492
Invent our own style:

25
00:01:24,492 --> 00:01:28,412
without references, probably ordinary,
and likely to be redone.

26
00:01:28,732 --> 00:01:30,695
Take the best parts of several apps.

27
00:01:31,015 --> 00:01:34,044
Or copy one mature app exactly,
with no mixing.

28
00:01:34,284 --> 00:01:35,299
Before the answer:

29
00:01:35,299 --> 00:01:39,190
why would copying one product beat
picking the best parts of several?

30
00:01:42,934 --> 00:01:45,825
Because parts
from different products disagree:

31
00:01:45,825 --> 00:01:48,225
spacing, colours, how a button behaves.

32
00:01:48,545 --> 00:01:52,105
The seams show,
and the decision record names a second cost:

33
00:01:52,105 --> 00:01:55,073
you can no longer say
where each choice came from.

34
00:01:55,393 --> 00:01:59,313
One mature reference answers
every small question the same way,

35
00:01:59,313 --> 00:02:03,670
so the fewest design calls give a page
that is neither ugly nor wrong.

36
00:02:03,990 --> 00:02:05,995
It also helps the people using it.

37
00:02:06,315 --> 00:02:08,173
Designers call it Jakob's law:

38
00:02:08,173 --> 00:02:11,022
users spend most of their time
on other sites,

39
00:02:11,022 --> 00:02:13,590
so they prefer yours to work the same way.

40
00:02:13,910 --> 00:02:18,603
The rule the agent proposed:
every screen copies exactly one reference.

41
00:02:18,923 --> 00:02:23,851
For the chat page, that is Claude's app,
which everyone on the team uses every day.

42
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:26,693
Here is what was copied.

43
00:02:27,013 --> 00:02:30,384
One column, at most 768 pixels wide.

44
00:02:30,704 --> 00:02:33,136
Your message in a beige bubble on the right.

45
00:02:33,456 --> 00:02:36,357
The answer in serif type,
full width, no frame.

46
00:02:36,600 --> 00:02:40,184
The composer:
a white rounded box that grows as you type.

47
00:02:40,504 --> 00:02:44,335
Along its bottom edge,
where Claude keeps attachments and tools,

48
00:02:44,335 --> 00:02:49,123
we put three drop-downs of our own:
who is asking, insurance, and where you are.

49
00:02:49,443 --> 00:02:52,804
On the right, the model menu,
Terra at medium effort,

50
00:02:52,804 --> 00:02:54,834
next to the terracotta send key,

51
00:02:54,834 --> 00:02:58,829
the same spot where Claude's help
page puts its own model menu.

52
00:02:59,067 --> 00:03:02,978
Two choices here were yours:
copy Claude's chat page at all,

53
00:03:02,978 --> 00:03:07,280
and copy its warm colours and serif
type too, not only the layout.

54
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:09,488
They stay inside this one page;

55
00:03:09,488 --> 00:03:12,229
the rest of the site
keeps its neutral theme.

56
00:03:13,567 --> 00:03:15,892
What was not copied, each for a reason.

57
00:03:16,212 --> 00:03:18,520
Claude's logo, name and private fonts:

58
00:03:18,520 --> 00:03:21,435
they aren't ours,
so where Claude shows its logo

59
00:03:21,435 --> 00:03:25,578
while it thinks, ours shows a small dot,
and the fonts are open ones.

60
00:03:25,898 --> 00:03:28,982
The sidebar,
chat history, attachments and tools:

61
00:03:28,982 --> 00:03:33,514
we have no accounts and no storage,
and the prototype doesn't need them.

62
00:03:33,834 --> 00:03:36,522
And the two from episode two:
the raw reasoning,

63
00:03:36,522 --> 00:03:38,762
and words streaming as they are written.

64
00:03:39,082 --> 00:03:41,130
Our answers pass the checker first.

65
00:03:42,467 --> 00:03:43,896
Now a harder question.

66
00:03:44,216 --> 00:03:48,163
A parent in China opens this page
about their child in Boston.

67
00:03:48,483 --> 00:03:50,680
What do they need that the student doesn't?

68
00:03:56,567 --> 00:03:58,082
Start with 911.

69
00:03:58,402 --> 00:04:00,386
A parent can't dial it from China.

70
00:04:00,706 --> 00:04:04,739
So every fixed line
on the page changes for parents:

71
00:04:04,739 --> 00:04:06,310
instead of call 911,

72
00:04:06,310 --> 00:04:10,711
it asks the child or someone
nearby to call 911 locally.

73
00:04:11,031 --> 00:04:14,530
Phone numbers get a plus one
and the words dial from China.

74
00:04:14,850 --> 00:04:18,729
Emergency and crisis numbers
get no dial link at all, only:

75
00:04:18,729 --> 00:04:20,439
ask someone there to call.

76
00:04:20,684 --> 00:04:22,561
Some additions are for everyone.

77
00:04:22,881 --> 00:04:26,433
In an emergency,
a red do-this-now block comes first,

78
00:04:26,433 --> 00:04:29,985
appears at once,
and is read aloud by screen readers.

79
00:04:30,305 --> 00:04:33,591
An emergency link sits
in the top bar at all times.

80
00:04:33,911 --> 00:04:36,656
Demos and estimates are labelled simulated,

81
00:04:36,656 --> 00:04:39,783
and when a student copies an answer
for a parent,

82
00:04:39,783 --> 00:04:42,337
the label goes with it: not a diagnosis.

83
00:04:42,584 --> 00:04:46,057
And some are for people
who use the page differently:

84
00:04:46,057 --> 00:04:49,268
hidden headings a screen reader
can jump between,

85
00:04:49,268 --> 00:04:53,332
source links at least 24 pixels tall
so a finger can hit them,

86
00:04:53,332 --> 00:04:58,968
and at 400 percent zoom, a page that opens
at the top with the emergency link in view.

87
00:05:00,317 --> 00:05:01,832
How did the agent build it?

88
00:05:02,152 --> 00:05:04,959
On Wednesday at one fifty-nine
in the morning,

89
00:05:04,959 --> 00:05:07,400
Boston time, it wrote the decision down.

90
00:05:07,720 --> 00:05:12,114
Seventeen minutes later it committed
the rebuilt page: 27 files.

91
00:05:12,434 --> 00:05:14,952
Six minutes after that, end-to-end tests.

92
00:05:15,200 --> 00:05:16,565
Then came the reviews.

93
00:05:16,885 --> 00:05:18,741
An independent visual review.

94
00:05:19,061 --> 00:05:23,328
Streaming, which you had asked for,
built as progress only, and a review of it.

95
00:05:23,648 --> 00:05:26,883
A second visual review,
which brought Claude's phone layout

96
00:05:26,883 --> 00:05:30,283
and much of the parent wording
and accessibility you just saw.

97
00:05:30,603 --> 00:05:34,344
And a final review from five angles:
about 30 problems,

98
00:05:34,344 --> 00:05:37,814
fixed on parallel branches,
then checked once more.

99
00:05:38,050 --> 00:05:39,906
One bug shows why this is hard.

100
00:05:40,226 --> 00:05:44,386
Send and stop share one button:
while you wait, send turns into stop.

101
00:05:44,706 --> 00:05:46,237
Click stop, the wait ends,

102
00:05:46,237 --> 00:05:49,476
and in that same click the button turns back
into send,

103
00:05:49,476 --> 00:05:51,597
so whatever was in the box went out.

104
00:05:51,917 --> 00:05:55,543
In a demo replay, that was the very
message you had just stopped.

105
00:05:55,863 --> 00:05:57,293
The fix was one line.

106
00:05:57,613 --> 00:05:59,802
Then Bugbot found the mirror image:

107
00:05:59,802 --> 00:06:03,181
double-click send,
and the second click lands on stop.

108
00:06:03,501 --> 00:06:07,405
Another one-line fix:
ignore the second click of a double-click.

109
00:06:07,725 --> 00:06:10,818
Copying how a button looks
does not copy how it behaves.

110
00:06:12,167 --> 00:06:14,279
What do the big chat apps
have that we don't?

111
00:06:14,599 --> 00:06:18,443
Conversation history and projects,
file uploads, voice,

112
00:06:18,443 --> 00:06:22,706
a side panel for documents and code,
and memory across chats.

113
00:06:23,026 --> 00:06:27,250
Most of them need accounts and storage,
which this prototype doesn't have.

114
00:06:27,570 --> 00:06:29,828
Of these, voice may matter most here,

115
00:06:29,828 --> 00:06:33,124
for a worried parent
who would rather speak than type;

116
00:06:33,124 --> 00:06:36,724
history and memory would mean
keeping health conversations,

117
00:06:36,724 --> 00:06:38,983
which needs a privacy decision first.

118
00:06:39,217 --> 00:06:40,732
And what could only we build?

119
00:06:41,052 --> 00:06:45,635
One answer for two readers, the student's
and the parent's, side by side;

120
00:06:45,635 --> 00:06:49,905
every answer already carries
a folded summary for parents, the seed.

121
00:06:50,225 --> 00:06:54,790
A summary card the student sends home;
copy for parent is already a first step.

122
00:06:55,110 --> 00:06:56,693
And a hand-off to booking:

123
00:06:56,693 --> 00:07:00,042
the answer already writes
the English sentences to say;

124
00:07:00,042 --> 00:07:03,110
the page could place the call,
or fill in the form.

125
00:07:03,350 --> 00:07:06,273
And what waits for you: a dozen open items.

126
00:07:06,593 --> 00:07:08,779
Decision six is still a proposal,

127
00:07:08,779 --> 00:07:12,753
and so is what the agent added
to it that night, unattended:

128
00:07:12,753 --> 00:07:16,065
progress-only streaming,
the model menu and retry.

129
00:07:16,385 --> 00:07:20,985
Two more choices need your yes:
a stopped message is no longer taken back

130
00:07:20,985 --> 00:07:23,028
but goes out with your next one,

131
00:07:23,028 --> 00:07:25,964
and parents get no dial link
for crisis lines.

132
00:07:27,300 --> 00:07:30,259
So: one reference per screen,
copied exactly,

133
00:07:30,259 --> 00:07:34,468
so every small choice has an answer
and the page feels familiar.

134
00:07:34,788 --> 00:07:38,137
Claude's layout, composer and colours,
kept to this page.

135
00:07:38,457 --> 00:07:43,001
Not its logo, its history, its raw thoughts
or its word-by-word streaming.

136
00:07:43,321 --> 00:07:47,034
Plus what only a medical page
for two audiences needs:

137
00:07:47,034 --> 00:07:50,747
emergency first,
wording a parent in China can act on,

138
00:07:50,747 --> 00:07:53,497
and labels that travel with copied text.

139
00:07:53,733 --> 00:07:54,842
Three questions.

140
00:07:55,162 --> 00:07:57,466
Why copy one product instead of several?

141
00:07:57,786 --> 00:08:01,328
Why does the parent's page
ask someone else to call 911?

142
00:08:01,648 --> 00:08:04,059
And what turned the send key
into a bug, twice?

143
00:08:07,800 --> 00:08:10,019
Next time: the two days themselves.

144
00:08:10,339 --> 00:08:13,411
What the agents did,
hour by hour, and what it cost.

